---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="The Complete Wire Mesh Procurement Checklist: RFQ to Receipt"
  description="Complete wire mesh procurement checklist covering all 8 stages: RFQ, supplier verification, sampling, contract, inspection, shipping, documents, and receipt."
  lang="en"
>
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-8">
    <nav class="text-sm text-theme-secondary mb-6">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-theme-primary">Home</a>
      <span class="mx-2">›</span>
      <a href="/blog/" class="hover:text-theme-primary">Blog</a>
      <span class="mx-2">›</span>
      <span>Wire Mesh Procurement Checklist</span>
    </nav>

    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">
      The Complete Wire Mesh Procurement Checklist: RFQ to Receipt
    </h1>

    <p class="text-theme-secondary text-lg mb-8">
      Last updated: September 2026 | 13 min read
    </p>

    <!-- Answer First -->
    <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-8">
      <p class="text-theme-primary text-lg leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Quick answer:</strong> Wire mesh procurement succeeds in eight stages, and each has a short list of items you check off before moving forward: <strong>(1) Specification</strong>—complete spec sheet with material grade, mesh count, wire diameter with tolerance, dimensions, coating weight, and standard; <strong>(2) Supplier shortlist</strong>—3–5 verified factories, business license checked, real-manufacturer confirmed; <strong>(3) Sampling</strong>—samples tested for mesh count, wire diameter, material grade, and coating before approval; <strong>(4) Contract</strong>—proforma invoice with fixed price, tolerances, AQL, penalty clauses, and payment terms; <strong>(5) Production monitoring</strong>—DUPRO inspection on orders above $20,000; <strong>(6) Pre-shipment inspection</strong>—independent PSI before the balance payment; <strong>(7) Shipping</strong>—packing spec, loading plan, five photo checkpoints, full document set; <strong>(8) Receipt</strong>—count, weigh, measure, and test against the PO within 48 hours. The full checkbox list, tolerance table, and document checklist are below.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- TOC -->
    <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Table of Contents</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-theme-secondary">
        <li><a href="#how-to-use" class="hover:text-theme-primary">How to Use This Checklist</a></li>
        <li><a href="#stages" class="hover:text-theme-primary">The 8-Stage Procurement Checklist</a></li>
        <li><a href="#tolerances" class="hover:text-theme-primary">Tolerance and Specification Reference Table</a></li>
        <li><a href="#documents" class="hover:text-theme-primary">Document Checklist: What You Must Receive</a></li>
        <li><a href="#mistakes" class="hover:text-theme-primary">Five Mistakes This Checklist Prevents</a></li>
        <li><a href="#faq" class="hover:text-theme-primary">FAQ</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- How to use -->
    <section id="how-to-use" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">How to Use This Checklist</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        This is a gate checklist: do not advance to the next stage until every box in the current stage is checked. Skipping a stage to save a week is how a 30-day delay becomes a 90-day dispute. Print it, paste it into your procurement workflow, or copy the checkbox items into your RFQ and PO templates.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Two tools referenced throughout: the <a href="/tools/inquiry-template-generator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">inquiry template generator</a> builds your Stage 1–2 RFQ document, and the <a href="/spec-tables/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">spec tables library</a> provides the standardized dimensions and tolerance values to copy into it. For strategic context on why these stages exist, see the <a href="/sourcing-guide/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">sourcing guide</a> and the <a href="/procurement-process/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">procurement process</a> overview.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 8 stages -->
    <section id="stages" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">The 8-Stage Procurement Checklist</h2>

      <!-- Stage 1 -->
      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Stage 1 — Specification and Internal Alignment</h3>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Product type written in standard terms (woven / welded / hexagonal / chain link / expanded / perforated)</li>
            <li>☐ Material grade specified with standard (e.g., 304 per ASTM A580, Q195/Q235 low-carbon for galvanized)</li>
            <li>☐ Mesh count in wires per inch AND aperture in mm</li>
            <li>☐ Wire diameter in mm with tolerance (reference table below)</li>
            <li>☐ Weave type for woven mesh (plain, twill, dutch)</li>
            <li>☐ Dimensions: roll/panel width × length, edge finish</li>
          </ul>
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Coating specified with weight (e.g., hot-dip zinc ≥40 g/m²; Z275; Galfan ZA95)</li>
            <li>☐ Testing standard referenced (ASTM E11 / ISO 9044 woven; ASTM A185 welded)</li>
            <li>☐ Quantity, theoretical weight, and weight tolerance stated</li>
            <li>☐ Packaging and labeling requirements attached</li>
            <li>☐ Certifications required (mill test report, SGS, ISO 9001)</li>
            <li>☐ Photo or drawing of target product attached to the RFQ</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Stage 2 -->
      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Stage 2 — Supplier Sourcing and Verification</h3>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ 3–5 suppliers identified (B2B platforms, factory directory, referrals)</li>
            <li>☐ Same RFQ document sent to all suppliers (built with the inquiry template generator)</li>
            <li>☐ Business license received and Unified Social Credit Code verified</li>
            <li>☐ Business scope includes manufacturing</li>
          </ul>
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Live video factory tour completed</li>
            <li>☐ Bank account beneficiary name matches company name</li>
            <li>☐ Mill certificates from recent production received</li>
            <li>☐ Quotes normalized to $/kg before comparison</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Stage 3 -->
      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Stage 3 — Sampling and Approval</h3>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Samples ordered from top 2–3 suppliers</li>
            <li>☐ Mesh count verified (mesh gauge or wire count)</li>
            <li>☐ Wire diameter measured with micrometer at 5+ points</li>
            <li>☐ Material grade confirmed (magnet screen + XRF lab test where critical)</li>
          </ul>
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Coating weight/thickness verified</li>
            <li>☐ Sample tested in the actual end application</li>
            <li>☐ Approved sample retained and reference number recorded</li>
            <li>☐ PO states mass production must match approved sample</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Stage 4 -->
      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Stage 4 — Contract and Payment Terms</h3>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Proforma invoice contains full specification from Stage 1</li>
            <li>☐ Price fixed, inclusive of packaging and export charges</li>
            <li>☐ Incoterm and named port stated (FOB Xingang recommended)</li>
            <li>☐ Payment terms: 30/70 T/T or better; never above 30% deposit</li>
          </ul>
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Tolerances and AQL written into the contract (AQL 2.5 major / 4.0 minor)</li>
            <li>☐ Late-delivery penalty clause (0.5–1% per week, capped at 10%)</li>
            <li>☐ Inspection rights and re-work/remake terms stated</li>
            <li>☐ Dispute jurisdiction and governing language agreed</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Stage 5 -->
      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Stage 5 — Production Monitoring</h3>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Production start date confirmed after deposit</li>
            <li>☐ Mid-production photo/video check requested (wire on creels, weaving/welding running)</li>
            <li>☐ DUPRO inspection booked for orders above $20,000</li>
          </ul>
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Material certificates for the actual production batch requested</li>
            <li>☐ Production timeline tracked against contract lead time</li>
            <li>☐ Deviations flagged in writing within 48 hours of discovery</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Stage 6 -->
      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Stage 6 — Pre-Shipment Inspection</h3>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Independent PSI booked (SGS, Bureau Veritas, QIMA, or Anping agency)</li>
            <li>☐ Inspector received the contract, spec, and approved sample reference</li>
            <li>☐ Mesh count, wire diameter, dimensions, and coating verified on sampled rolls</li>
            <li>☐ XRF spot-check on material grade (1 roll per 10)</li>
          </ul>
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Quantity and roll count checked against packing list</li>
            <li>☐ Packaging checked against spec (waterproof wrap, straps, edge protectors, labels)</li>
            <li>☐ Container loading photos taken (empty / 25% / 75% / full / sealed)</li>
            <li>☐ Balance payment released only after passing PSI report</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Stage 7 -->
      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Stage 7 — Shipping and Documents</h3>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Booking made with forwarder; sailing schedule confirmed</li>
            <li>☐ Loading plan verified against container calculator output</li>
            <li>☐ Moisture protection on board (VCI/desiccants) per transit length</li>
            <li>☐ Container weight within payload and road limits</li>
          </ul>
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Commercial invoice received and checked (values, HS codes, incoterm)</li>
            <li>☐ Packing list cross-checked against PO</li>
            <li>☐ Bill of lading instructions sent (original or telex release)</li>
            <li>☐ Certificate of Origin and inspection report requested</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Stage 8 -->
      <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Stage 8 — Receipt, Verification, and Close-Out</h3>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Container photos taken before opening (seal number, exterior)</li>
            <li>☐ Interior photographed before unloading</li>
            <li>☐ Roll/pallet count verified against packing list</li>
            <li>☐ Weighbridge or scale check on total consignment</li>
          </ul>
          <ul class="space-y-2">
            <li>☐ Sampled rolls micrometer-checked for wire diameter and coating</li>
            <li>☐ Any damage noted on delivery receipt and reported within 24–48 hours</li>
            <li>☐ Supplier performance scored (quality, lead time, communication)</li>
            <li>☐ Lessons and spec updates fed back into Stage 1 template</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Tolerances -->
    <section id="tolerances" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Tolerance and Specification Reference Table</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Tolerances that are not written down will not be honored. Copy these into your PO; they reflect common commercial practice for export-grade mesh and the verification methods your inspector will use. Product families differ—<a href="/products/welded-wire-mesh/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">welded wire mesh panels</a> are checked per panel, rolls per sampled roll.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Parameter</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Standard Tolerance</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Verification Method</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Contract Wording</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Wire diameter</td>
              <td class="p-3">±0.01 mm under 0.5 mm; ±3% above 0.5 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">Micrometer (0.01 mm), 5 points per roll</td>
              <td class="p-3">"Wire diameter per spec ±3%, measured at any point"</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Mesh count / aperture</td>
              <td class="p-3">±3% of nominal</td>
              <td class="p-3">Mesh counter gauge, 3 positions per roll</td>
              <td class="p-3">"Mesh count within ±3% across full roll width"</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Roll / panel width</td>
              <td class="p-3">±10 mm (rolls); ±5 mm (panels)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Steel tape, 3 points across</td>
              <td class="p-3">"Width within ±10 mm at any measurement point"</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Roll length</td>
              <td class="p-3">0% / +5% (never short)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Unroll-measure or weight-based verification</td>
              <td class="p-3">"Length not less than stated, overage max 5%"</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Panel flatness (welded)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Max 5 mm deviation on 1 m straightedge</td>
              <td class="p-3">Straightedge + feeler gauge</td>
              <td class="p-3">"Flatness within 5 mm/m on flat surface"</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Zinc coating weight</td>
              <td class="p-3">Per stated class (e.g., Z40 min 40 g/m², Z275 min 275 g/m²)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Coating thickness gauge or gravimetric strip test</td>
              <td class="p-3">"Coating class Z__, verified per batch"</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Material grade</td>
              <td class="p-3">Exact grade, no substitution (304, 316L, etc.)</td>
              <td class="p-3">XRF analyzer, 1 roll per 10 sampled</td>
              <td class="p-3">"Grade per __ standard; XRF-verified; substitution = full refund"</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Quantity (count and weight)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Count: 0% short. Weight: −0% / +5% vs theoretical</td>
              <td class="p-3">Physical count; weighbridge ticket</td>
              <td class="p-3">"Quantity and theoretical weight verified before balance payment"</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        The last row matters most in practice: short shipment and underweight rolls are the two most common quantity failures in this trade, and both are fully preventable with a weighbridge ticket and a count at the PSI stage—before the balance leaves your account.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Documents -->
    <section id="documents" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Document Checklist: What You Must Receive</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Documents are the paper trail that customs, your bank, your insurer, and—if it comes to it—arbitration will all ask for. Collect the full set for every order; chasing a missing Certificate of Origin after the ship sails is far harder than requesting it at booking time.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Document</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Issued By</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Used For</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Check At Receipt</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Commercial Invoice</td>
              <td class="p-3">Factory</td>
              <td class="p-3">Customs valuation, payment, insurance</td>
              <td class="p-3">Values, HS codes, incoterm, bank details match PO</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Packing List</td>
              <td class="p-3">Factory</td>
              <td class="p-3">Receiving count, freight check</td>
              <td class="p-3">Roll/pallet counts, gross/net weights vs theoretical</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Bill of Lading (B/L)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Shipping line</td>
              <td class="p-3">Title to goods; customs release</td>
              <td class="p-3">Shipper/consignee, ports, container and seal numbers; original vs telex release</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Certificate of Origin (CoO)</td>
              <td class="p-3">CCPIT / chamber</td>
              <td class="p-3">Preferential tariffs, import compliance</td>
              <td class="p-3">Correct scheme (e.g., RCEP/Form A where applicable), matches invoice</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Mill Test Certificate / MTC</td>
              <td class="p-3">Steel mill via factory</td>
              <td class="p-3">Material grade proof (chemistry, mechanical)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Heat number traceable to the production batch</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Inspection Report (PSI/DUPRO)</td>
              <td class="p-3">SGS / BV / QIMA / agency</td>
              <td class="p-3">Quality evidence, claims, balance-payment gate</td>
              <td class="p-3">Measurements, photos, pass/fail per AQL; dated before balance paid</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Insurance Certificate</td>
              <td class="p-3">Insurer / forwarder</td>
              <td class="p-3">Cargo claims</td>
              <td class="p-3">Coverage value ≥110% of CIF, correct voyage and goods description</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        File the set per PO—invoice, packing list, B/L, CoO, MTC, inspection report, insurance certificate—indexed by PO number. Six months later, when a rust complaint or a duty audit arrives, this folder is the difference between a two-hour answer and a two-week scramble.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Mistakes -->
    <section id="mistakes" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Five Mistakes This Checklist Prevents</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4 mb-6">
        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">1. The two-line RFQ</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            "Stainless mesh 100 mesh, 10 rolls" invites the factory to choose the cheapest interpretation of every unstated parameter. Stage 1's twelve items take twenty minutes to write and eliminate the most common disputes entirely.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">2. Comparing quotes in different units</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            Per-roll, per-m², and per-panel quotes cannot be compared until normalized to $/kg. Stage 2 forces the conversion; without it, the "cheapest" quote routinely contains the least steel.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">3. Paying the balance before inspection</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            Once the balance is paid, your leverage drops to nearly zero. Stage 6 makes the balance payment conditional on a passed inspection report—protecting quality at the exact moment it is still enforceable.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">4. Accepting "industry standard" tolerances</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            There is no enforceable industry-standard tolerance unless it is in your contract. The tolerance table above converts polite assumptions into contractual language with named verification methods.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">5. Discovering short shipment at installation</h3>
          <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
            Counting rolls against the packing list at container opening (Stage 8) and a weighbridge check take under an hour. Finding the shortage three months later, mid-project, makes it an argument instead of a claim.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Can I skip the pre-shipment inspection on repeat orders?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            You can reduce it, but do not eliminate it. After 3–4 clean cycles with the same factory and unchanged spec, a photo-and-video inspection from the factory plus a random third-party visit every few orders is reasonable. Keep the balance-payment gate in place regardless—only the structure changes. Any spec change, new material grade, or gap over six months resets you to full PSI.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What is a realistic total procurement timeline using this checklist?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            For a first order with a new supplier: 1–2 weeks for Stages 1–3 (spec, sourcing, samples), 1 week for Stage 4 (contract), 2–4 weeks Stage 5 (production), about 1 week for Stage 6 (inspection and booking), then 3–5 weeks ocean transit plus ~1 week customs for Stages 7–8. Total: 8–10 weeks. Repeat orders with approved specs typically compress to 4–7 weeks.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Which checklist items matter most for stainless steel mesh specifically?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Material grade verification (Stage 3 XRF test and Stage 6 spot checks), because 201-grade substitution is the single most expensive scam in stainless mesh. Also moisture protection in Stage 7—304 arriving wet develops tea staining that customers mistake for rust. Keep the MTC with a traceable heat number in the document folder; it is your proof in any grade dispute.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">How do I handle a supplier that refuses inspection rights in the contract?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Treat refusal of third-party inspection as a disqualifying red flag, especially combined with pressure for a large deposit. Legitimate Anping factories accept inspection as standard practice—it is written into most of their export contracts already. A supplier who will not be inspected is telling you what the inspection would find.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
